# Terminus Edition 2 — Dockerfile & Image Best Practices

**Canonical summary:** `.cursor/rules/terminus.mdc` section **Dockerfile and image best practices** (main file).

This file is the extended Snorkel **Dockerfile & Image Best Practices** (15 sections). Apply on every task audit and creation. On conflict with other Terminus rules, prefer the **stricter** rule or platform CI output.

All Terminal-Bench task images must be:

| Property | Requirement |
|----------|-------------|
| **Reproducible** | Same source builds to the same image over time and across systems |
| **Cacheable** | Common layers are shared across tasks |
| **Lazy-pull friendly** | Startup-critical files are accessible without pulling the full image |
| **Auditable** | Images are digest-pinned, signed, labeled, and free of secrets |
| **Complete** | Tasks run without network access — all dependencies in the image |
| **Siloed** | Image must not leak task solutions or tests |
| **Resourced** | CPU, memory, and storage defined in `task.toml` |

---

## 1. Pin base images by digest

Every `FROM` line must use an immutable digest. Never use floating tags such as `latest`. Tags may be included for readability, but the **digest is the source of truth**. CI fails any `FROM` without `@sha256`.

```dockerfile
# Bad
FROM python:3.13-slim-bookworm

# Good
FROM python:3.13-slim-bookworm@sha256:...
```

Update digests deliberately, in reviewable commits.

---

## 2. Use sanctioned base images where possible

Prefer Reflection-sanctioned Terminal-Bench base images over ad hoc public images. A good base-image family provides shell utilities, observability tooling (if required), standard CA certs/locale, common verifier/runtime bootstrap tools, and a pinned package-manager baseline.

Runtime classes: Python, Node, JVM, Rust/C/C++ build, database/service, minimal POSIX.

Custom base images must be flagged to Reflection ahead of time and should generally be avoided.

---

## 3. Make builds reproducible

Pin all dependencies; avoid nondeterministic build steps.

| Ecosystem | Lockfiles |
|-----------|-----------|
| Python | `requirements.txt` (exact versions), `uv.lock`, `poetry.lock` |
| Node | `package-lock.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn.lock` |
| Rust | `Cargo.lock` |
| Go | `go.mod` and **`go.sum`** |
| JVM | Pinned Maven/Gradle locks where possible |

Pin downloaded binaries by version and checksum. Avoid `curl | sh` unless version-pinned and checksum-verified:

```dockerfile
# Bad
RUN curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh

# Good
ARG UV_VERSION=0.6.14
ARG UV_SHA256=...
RUN curl -fsSL "https://github.com/astral-sh/uv/releases/download/${UV_VERSION}/uv-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz" -o /tmp/uv.tar.gz \
    && echo "${UV_SHA256}  /tmp/uv.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c - \
    && tar -xzf /tmp/uv.tar.gz -C /usr/local/bin --strip-components=1 \
    && rm /tmp/uv.tar.gz
```

Avoid build steps that embed wall-clock time, random paths, usernames, or nondeterministic generated output. Set `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` where applicable.

---

## 4. Structure layers from least volatile to most volatile

Order Dockerfile operations so task edits only invalidate top layers.

- Base OS and runtime dependencies **before** task-specific source
- Copy manifest files **before** copying source
- Install dependencies **before** copying frequently edited files
- Do **not** use `COPY . /app` unless the task directory is minimal and `.dockerignore` is strict
- Aim for purposeful layers (not one squashed layer)

```dockerfile
FROM <base>@sha256:...

# 1. OS/system packages
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ... \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# 2. Runtime / package-manager setup
COPY requirements.txt /tmp/requirements.txt
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r /tmp/requirements.txt

# 3–6. Manifests, deps, source, task build
...
WORKDIR /app
```

---

## 5. Consolidate apt usage correctly

One apt transaction per stage and volatility class.

- Do **not** run `apt-get upgrade`
- Do **not** leave `/var/lib/apt/lists/*` in the image
- Do **not** install recommended packages unless required
- Do **not** install build tools in the runtime stage unless the task requires them
- Prefer common apt packages in sanctioned base images

```dockerfile
# Bad
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y git
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y tmux asciinema

# Good
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        asciinema \
        ca-certificates \
        git \
        python3 \
        python3-pip \
        tmux \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
```

---

## 6. Keep build tools out of runtime images (when possible)

Use **multi-stage builds** when the Dockerfile compiles or bundles artifacts. If the task requires the agent to compile or build code, toolchains in the final image are fine.

Use multi-stage for: `cargo build`, `go build`, `mvn package`, `gradle build`, `npm ci && npm run build`, `dotnet publish`, C/C++ with make/cmake, etc.

---

## 7. Images must contain all dependencies — app **and** verifier

All tasks must work **without network** during agent run and verifier.

**Mandatory:**

- **`tmux`** and **`asciinema`** must be installed — agent runtime requires both; missing either causes agent runs to fail with no verifier output
- All **app-runtime** package downloads happen at **image build time** (the libs/binaries the agent's code actually uses, pinned in lockfiles)
- **Test-only** packages (pytest, pytest-json-ctrf, numpy, Playwright, Vitest, etc.) are built into an **isolated venv at `/opt/verifier-venv`** at build time (`RUN python3 -m venv /opt/verifier-venv && /opt/verifier-venv/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir pytest==8.4.1 pytest-json-ctrf==0.3.5`; needs `python3-venv` in apt). They must **not** go in the system/app Python (`--break-system-packages` fails `test_deps_in_image`) and **not** as wheels under `tests/` (a blocker).
- `test.sh` runs `/opt/verifier-venv/bin/python -m pytest` — it must **not** `pip install`, create a venv, or use `curl`, `wget`, `npm install`, `cargo fetch`, `mvn dependency:get`, or similar networked operations at runtime
- The Docker **build** may reach the network; the **runtime** stays offline. `tests/` holds only test scripts — no `wheels/`, no `.whl`
- `task.toml`: **`allow_internet = false`**
- Oracle must pass with network disabled

---

## 8. Separate agent-visible runtime from verifier-only assets

- Do **not** copy `solution/` into the runtime image
- Do **not** copy `tests/` into the runtime image (the verifier mounts it at runtime)
- Do **not** copy hidden tests into agent-visible locations
- Do **not** store expected outputs in writable agent paths
- Do **not** let the verifier read ground truth from agent-modifiable files
- Verifier-only packages (pytest, pytest-json-ctrf) **are** installed in the image, pinned. Do **not** ship `.whl` files or a `wheels/` directory under `tests/` — that is a blocker
- Public fixtures may be in the image; hidden verifier assets stay isolated

---

## 9. Use `.dockerignore` and narrow `COPY`

All non-trivial tasks must include `environment/.dockerignore`.

- Prefer `COPY file file` or `COPY src/ src/` over `COPY . .`
- Do not copy caches, virtualenvs, build outputs, `.git`, editor files, credentials

Recommended `.dockerignore`:

```
.git
.gitignore
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/node_modules/
**/target/
**/dist/
**/build/
**/.venv/
**/venv/
.env
*.log
solution/
tests/
```

---

## 10. Store files as files, not heredocs or opaque archives

```dockerfile
# Bad
RUN cat > /app/foo.py <<'EOF'
print('hello world')
EOF

# Good
COPY hello_world.py /app/hello_world.py
```

Extract archives at build time so lazy-pull works:

```dockerfile
COPY fixtures.tar.gz /tmp/fixtures.tar.gz
RUN mkdir -p /app/fixtures \
    && tar -xzf /tmp/fixtures.tar.gz -C /app/fixtures \
    && rm /tmp/fixtures.tar.gz
```

---

## 11. Use metadata-preserving COPY options

Only change permissions where needed.

```dockerfile
# Bad
RUN chmod -R 755 /app

# Good
COPY --chmod=0755 run.sh /usr/local/bin/run-task
COPY --chown=appuser:appuser src/ /app/src/
```

---

## 12. General image hygiene

Unless required, images must **not** contain: `.git`, `.env`, credentials, SSH keys, package-manager/compiler caches, unused build tools, editor files, large unused datasets, hidden solution files.

Perform cleanup in the **same layer** that creates files:

```dockerfile
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends build-essential \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
```

---

## 13. Optimise for lazy pull and hot-path locality

Put startup-critical files in earlier, smaller layers: shell entrypoints, instructions, small runtime scripts, dependency metadata, small fixtures for the first command.

Keep large optional datasets out of the image when possible; mount at runtime instead.

---

## 14. Declare task resources explicitly

`task.toml` must include realistic `[environment]` resources from observed runs. Oracle must pass within limits.

```toml
[agent]
timeout_sec = 900

[verifier]
timeout_sec = 420

[environment]
build_timeout_sec = 600.0
cpus = 1
memory_mb = 2048
storage_mb = 10240
allow_internet = false
# no workdir on flat tasks — it is a milestone-only field
```

---

## 15. Publish images with audit metadata

```dockerfile
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.source="..."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.revision="..."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.created="..."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.version="..."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.licenses="..."
```

---

## Terminus verifier integration (`test.sh`)

`tests/test.sh` should:

- create `/logs/verifier`
- run `/opt/verifier-venv/bin/python -m pytest` (the venv is built at image time — no `pip install`, no venv at test time)
- capture the exit code
- write `0` or `1` to `/logs/verifier/reward.txt`

If a dependency is needed only by tests, **install it in `environment/Dockerfile`, pinned**. Do **not** ship it as a wheel under `tests/` — that is a blocker.

**Blockers in `test.sh`:** any `pip install`, `curl`, `wget`, `uv install`, `uvx` downloads, `npm install` from registry, `npx playwright install`, `apt-get install`, venv creation, any runtime download.

**Blockers in `environment/Dockerfile`:** verifier-only deps (pytest, pytest-json-ctrf) **missing** from the image; or `.whl`/`wheels/` shipped under `tests/`.

Default skeleton: `task.toml` has `allow_internet = false`; `environment/Dockerfile` builds an isolated `/opt/verifier-venv` with pinned pytest + pytest-json-ctrf (plus app deps + language runtime in the normal build); `tests/` holds only `test.sh` + `test_*.py`; `tests/test.sh` runs `/opt/verifier-venv/bin/python -m pytest`.

---

## Cursor verification checklist (Dockerfile audit)

Before saying Dockerfile setup is OK, confirm from **actual files**:

1. Every `FROM` in `environment/Dockerfile` uses `@sha256:...` (or flag as blocker for CI)
2. **`tmux`** and **`asciinema`** present in image (or flag High)
3. `task.toml` has `[environment] allow_internet = false` and **no `workdir` on flat tasks** (milestone-only field)
4. App deps pinned; lockfiles where applicable (`go.sum`, `package-lock.json`, etc.)
5. Verifier-only deps (pytest, pytest-json-ctrf) **installed and pinned in the image**; **no** `.whl`/`wheels/` under `tests/`
6. `environment/.dockerignore` exists for non-trivial tasks and excludes `tests/`
7. No `solution/` or `tests/` copied into the image
8. No secrets, solution hints, or ground-truth outputs in `environment/`
9. Layer order: manifests/deps before volatile source where possible
10. Do **not** claim CI-clean or digest-compliant unless digests were actually checked

Do not say "verifier dependency setup is okay" unless **all three** are true: no installs or network calls in `test.sh` (it runs pytest directly), verifier-only deps (pytest, pytest-json-ctrf) are installed and pinned in `environment/Dockerfile`, and `tests/` contains no `.whl`/`wheels/`.
